{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1420735972,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDIxNjAxMTI4Mg=="},"added":["annex"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1420740246,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDY5MjIwOTc3","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/32#issuecomment-69220977"},"message":"Yaroslav Halchenko wrote:\n\n\u003e 1. operations (even a simple du or ls -R) might take MUCH longer since leaf\n\u003e    directories are to contain a single file and thus there is quite a heavy\n\u003e    file hierarchy traversal is needed.\n\nI am doubtful about this without seeing some measurement.\n\nThe full traversal is .git/annex/xxx/yyy/KEY/obj, possibly with\n../../ in front. Does removing one item from this path really impact\nmuch? Note that the yyy directory is rarely going to be in cache either.\n\nAlso, any change in this area would break compatability with every\nexisting git-annex repository in a horrible way, as the path is\ncommitted to git. Rewriting all the paths would bloat the git repository\nsize, and leave files inaccessible when checking out old tags etc.\n\n## \n\nsee shy jo","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1420742051,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDY5MjI1NzUy","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/32#issuecomment-69225752"},"message":"indeed, yyy would unlikely to be in the cache ... but it is not just about the cache  -- next thought was if it was xxx/yy (or even xx/yy) then collisions in large repositories would lead to growing number of files under yy, while still allowing for efficient lookup and also making reading that directory content more efficient due to having multiple keys stored at that level.\nyeah -- this food for though is scary and might be just be highlighting the fact that a good middle ground could be hard to achieve throughout all the use cases and asking for supporting multiple incompatible layouts... but that is why I am experimenting/checking now before actually creating a bunch of annexes to share ;)\nanyways -- I will try to get some timing numbers to argue without empty hands -- it could well be that pros don't outweight cons ;-)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1420811348,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDY5MzM1OTk1","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/32#issuecomment-69335995"},"message":"just preliminary results for now.  only on ZFS with any ARC (1st or 2nd level) disabled since it is impossible afaik just to flush those read caches.  -xx-yy-key is the original git annex layout  and -xx-yy is where I moved files from their per-file directory.  annex keeps around 12,000 files .  Such a directory is one of the 500 I have and even managed to merge into a single annex I was talking about before ;)\n\n```\n*$\u003e ./timeit.sh 992774-xx-yy-key 992774-xx-yy\n= Command: du -scm 992774-xx-yy-key\n== Cold du -scm 992774-xx-yy-key\n1482    992774-xx-yy-key\n1482    total\n        1:54.20 real,   0.60 user,      22.03 sys\n== Warm/repeated\n1482    992774-xx-yy-key\n1482    total\n        1:19.53 real,   0.44 user,      10.40 sys\n= Command: du -scm 992774-xx-yy\n== Cold du -scm 992774-xx-yy\n724     992774-xx-yy\n724     total\n        1:04.68 real,   0.32 user,      15.70 sys\n== Warm/repeated\n724     992774-xx-yy\n724     total\n        0:39.24 real,   0.18 user,      5.54 sys\n\n\n= Command: tar -cf 992774-xx-yy-key.tgz 992774-xx-yy-key\n== Cold tar -cf 992774-xx-yy-key.tgz 992774-xx-yy-key\n        2:18.79 real,   3.23 user,      28.32 sys\n== Warm/repeated\n        1:31.54 real,   2.45 user,      13.09 sys\n= Command: tar -cf 992774-xx-yy.tgz 992774-xx-yy\n== Cold tar -cf 992774-xx-yy.tgz 992774-xx-yy\n        1:34.76 real,   1.94 user,      17.86 sys\n== Warm/repeated\n        1:08.95 real,   1.56 user,      7.84 sys\n\n\n= Command: chmod +w -R 992774-xx-yy-key/.git/annex/objects\n== Cold chmod +w -R 992774-xx-yy-key/.git/annex/objects\n        1:52.10 real,   0.44 user,      19.15 sys\n== Warm/repeated\n        1:19.14 real,   0.32 user,      10.68 sys\n= Command: chmod +w -R 992774-xx-yy/.git/annex/objects\n== Cold chmod +w -R 992774-xx-yy/.git/annex/objects\n        0:58.14 real,   0.27 user,      10.69 sys\n== Warm/repeated\n        0:39.73 real,   0.21 user,      5.22 sys\n\n\n= Command: rm -rf 992774-xx-yy-key\n== Cold rm -rf 992774-xx-yy-key\n        3:25.58 real,   0.54 user,      40.51 sys\n= Command: rm -rf 992774-xx-yy\n== Cold rm -rf 992774-xx-yy\n        1:41.23 real,   0.34 user,      18.96 sys\n```\n\nAs expected, all run times are cut by 1/3, besides that rm seems to be cut more than in half.  These timings were from a single run only.  Also amount of file system real estate, at least with this obscure test where files are actually tiny is cut nearly in half.  I expect also that on larger repos, with more collisions under xx/yy directories difference in performance will be even larger.  I might run such a test later on.\nIf interested, original location was smaug:/mnt/scrap/datalad/mimic_repos/hcp-ls.20141020-500subjects-addurl  and all the scripts used to produce those two directories and run these benchmarks are now available from https://github.com/datalad/datalad/tree/master/tools/benchmarking . I will generalize it all into a single command to be ran later on multiple locations","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1421018633,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDY5NTE3MjYx","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/32#issuecomment-69517261"},"message":"ok -- did a bit more of silly benchmarking which also seems have pointed to two weak RAM modules in the new box ;)  I have added also -xx-y  layout where for the 2nd level only one character is used, and then -deref -- as if data was never annex'ed (present only in the last results for 120,000 files): http://www.onerussian.com/tmp/benchs.txt .  So thing are pretty much boiling down to twice speed up on large repos from going away from xx/yy/key to xx/yy, and then also twice to allowing more collisions (of which there weren't way too many -- up to 9 -- with 120,000 files) via using just xx/y.   Since I have no plans for that box for this night -- let's make it sweat with 1,200,000 files... although not sure if it wouldn't be prohibitive for git without doing that \"merge\" trick with multiple repositories.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1421685518,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDcwNTIzMDM2","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/32#issuecomment-70523036"},"message":"@joeyh  -- here is that ultimate run for 1,200,000 files (above url on my website also should contain it).  Using an xx/y/ layout with more collisions (but not much really -- just goes up to few with 62 collisions) provides notable advantages in terms of size and performance impact\n\n```\nI: stats\ntest1200000-xx-yy-key:  size: 154091M   inodes: 8961913 links: 1200000  2nd level collisions: 1:381573 10:1 2:218490 3:83650 4:23750 5:5526 6:1078 7:163 8:22 9:8 \ntest1200000-xx-yy:      size: 35856M    inodes: 3116333 links: 1200000  2nd level collisions: 1:381573 10:1 2:218490 3:83650 4:23750 5:5526 6:1078 7:163 8:22 9:8 \ntest1200000-xx-y:       size: 4074M     inodes: 2434840 links: 1200000  2nd level collisions: 13:2 16:1 17:3 18:9 19:16 20:26 21:55 22:102 23:155 24:216 25:323 26:511 27:623 28:865 29:961 30:1300 31:1554 32:1620 33:1846 34:2029 35:2146 36:2123 37:2076 38:2101 39:1898 40:1813 41:1549 42:1381 43:1216 44:961 45:795 46:675 47:466 48:373 49:277 50:204 51:154 52:102 53:79 54:52 55:42 56:25 57:18 58:11 59:9 61:1 62:4 \ntest1200000-deref:      size: 618M      inodes: 1201002 links: 0    2nd level collisions: NA\n\n\n= Command: du -sm test1200000-deref \n== Cold: 618 test1200000-deref\n    0:52.29 real,   0.67 user,  26.85 sys\n== Warm: 618    test1200000-deref\n    0:10.64 real,   0.37 user,  4.30 sys\n= Command: du -sm test1200000-xx-y \n== Cold: 4074   test1200000-xx-y\n    1:46.54 real,   1.88 user,  67.19 sys\n== Warm: 4074   test1200000-xx-y\n    0:25.23 real,   0.88 user,  11.00 sys\n= Command: du -sm test1200000-xx-yy \n== Cold: 35856  test1200000-xx-yy\n    10:25.69 real,  7.57 user,  254.21 sys\n== Warm: 35856  test1200000-xx-yy\n    8:17.42 real,   8.26 user,  191.94 sys\n= Command: du -sm test1200000-xx-yy-key \n== Cold: 154091  test1200000-xx-yy-key\n    28:52.08 real,  29.51 user, 613.93 sys\n== Warm: 154091 test1200000-xx-yy-key\n    17:24.76 real,  21.01 user, 296.61 sys\n\n\n= Command: tar -cf test1200000-deref.tgz test1200000-deref \n== Cold:    1:39.41 real,   3.86 user,  62.84 sys\n== Warm:    0:45.35 real,   2.83 user,  33.19 sys\n= Command: tar -cf test1200000-xx-y.tgz test1200000-xx-y \n== Cold:    5:16.26 real,   62.94 user, 174.10 sys\n== Warm:    3:18.12 real,   61.89 user, 108.01 sys\n= Command: tar -cf test1200000-xx-yy.tgz test1200000-xx-yy \n== Cold:    13:57.58 real,  93.17 user, 358.36 sys\n== Warm:    10:12.85 real,  80.26 user, 232.72 sys\n= Command: tar -cf test1200000-xx-yy-key.tgz test1200000-xx-yy-key \n== Cold:    1:43:01 real,   257.89 user,    1750.94 sys\n== Warm:    40:53.03 real,  158.88 user,    948.32 sys\n\n\n= Command: chmod +w -R test1200000-xx-y/.git/annex/objects \n== Cold:    1:43.88 real,   1.23 user,  62.97 sys\n== Warm:    0:17.84 real,   0.24 user,  17.19 sys\n= Command: chmod +w -R test1200000-xx-yy/.git/annex/objects \n== Cold:    9:53.64 real,   7.98 user,  259.94 sys\n== Warm:    8:25.23 real,   6.14 user,  213.53 sys\n= Command: chmod +w -R test1200000-xx-yy-key/.git/annex/objects \n== Cold:    22:19.55 real,  17.09 user, 501.07 sys\n== Warm:    18:10.13 real,  14.58 user, 349.21 sys\n\n\n= Command: rm -rf test1200000-deref \n== Cold:    1:37.02 real,   0.65 user,  81.36 sys\n= Command: rm -rf test1200000-xx-y \n== Cold:    4:05.82 real,   1.99 user,  207.38 sys\n= Command: rm -rf test1200000-xx-yy \n== Cold:    14:09.17 real,  10.73 user, 451.76 sys\n= Command: rm -rf test1200000-xx-yy-key \n== Cold:    44:52.93 real,  31.27 user, 1274.58 sys\n```","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1421841347,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDcwODI1MDIw","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/33#issuecomment-70825020"},"message":"This is a symptom that we could see frequently, probably not every time coming with a nice workaround.\n\nIn my opinion this leads to a basic design decision: do we want people to work in annex'd directories?\n\nIf not, then we could use a \"crawler\"-like behavior for the generation and update of dataset handles. I.e. a sentinel job monitors a configured directory and reflects changes in the annex, but leaves the original directory untouched. Instead of a constant monitoring it could also be round-based and hand-triggered (\"now I am ready for revision 2\").\n\nIt get's more complicate on the consumption side of dataset handles. I obtain a handle `git annex get` its content, and get it served in an un-annexed directory, where its content is hard-linked into the actual annex. git annex could then monitor the \"detached checkout\" to identify modified/added/removed files. I have no idea how this would work in combination with meta-data based views of a dataset.\n\nNeeds more thinking...","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1421850719,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDcwODQ2NTcz","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/33#issuecomment-70846573"},"message":"also do not forget about \"direct mode\".  For datasets managed exclusively with datalad, and thus git-annex, we might escape all the symlinks madness by simply using direct mode \"installations\". Side effect -- without \"lean view\" ( #25 ) there would be empty (0 size) files present.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1421940748,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDcxMDM4MzI2","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/33#issuecomment-71038326"},"message":"Yeah, direct mode is there. And maybe that is what we should think about as a default mode of operation. Benjamin brought up that the git annex setup as a whole is more complicated on windows. Same goes for mapping possible workflows fro. Linux to windows. Maybe we should constrain our thinking to what is possible on windows right away? The thought alone pains me...","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1421951989,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDcxMDczNTU4","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/33#issuecomment-71073558"},"message":"Let me figure things out before considering such painful thoughts. The\nproblems I experience on Windows give me a fast track to a deeper insight.\nSo that's the upside for now. As far as I can see for now, the problem is:\nUsing direct mode leads to the question Michael brought up:\n\nIn my opinion this leads to a basic design decision: do we want people to\n\n\u003e work in annex'd directories?\n\nI have little experience with the targeted kind of users but I'm afraid\nforcing users to do or not to do it this way may be lead to low acceptance\nand lots of complains. Am I wrong here?\n\n2015-01-22 16:32 GMT+01:00 Michael Hanke notifications@github.com:\n\n\u003e Yeah, direct mode is there. And maybe that is what we should think about\n\u003e as a default mode of operation. Benjamin brought up that the git annex\n\u003e setup as a whole is more complicated on windows. Same goes for mapping\n\u003e possible workflows fro. Linux to windows. Maybe we should constrain our\n\u003e thinking to what is possible on windows right away? The thought alone pains\n\u003e me...\n\u003e \n\u003e —\n\u003e Reply to this email directly or view it on GitHub\n\u003e https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/33#issuecomment-71038326.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1455315670,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDE4MzUwOTkzMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/33#issuecomment-183509930"},"message":"IMHO we should just have a documentation section outlining such gotchas and workarounds (env var for AFNI, direct mode resolution), thus marking as documentation and assigning to @hanke ;)","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1455315681,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDU0OTE0MTQxMg=="},"added":["documentation"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1522687738,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM3Nzk3NTQwNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/33#issuecomment-377975407"},"message":"When #2357 is merged, move to https://github.com/datalad/datalad-neuroimaging","files":null}]}